From b82adefd9664c73a0fd74af0684b501db3173ebb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Taehyun Kim <dice.thkim@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 3 Sep 2026 14:30:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 02/18] SCUMM: Render Korean hi-res text with a TrueType font

Builds on the hi-res text mode by replacing the upscaled bitmap glyphs
with real TrueType output, which is what the extra resolution was for:
scaling an 11x12 bitmap to 2x only produces 2x2 pixel blocks, whereas a
TTF rasterised at 24px carries actual detail.

Rendering uses kTTFRenderModeMonochrome so the glyphs stay compatible
with the CLUT8 text surface and its 0xFD transparency mask; no alpha
blending is involved. Characters are mapped from CP949 to Unicode with
convertUHCToUCS(), i.e. ScummVM's own encoding.dat tables -- no iconv
dependency is introduced.

Two details were needed to make this work:

- The Korean multi-font system swaps _2byteHeight whenever the game
  changes charset (12 pixels for dialogue, 8 for the interface). A
  single TTF instance would therefore overflow the line box of the
  smaller charsets and make consecutive lines collide, so instances are
  cached per line box and selected on demand.

- Double-byte glyphs now always go to the (scaled) text surface, even
  for virtual screens which normally receive text directly, such as the
  verb area. That surface is the only buffer with enough resolution to
  hold them. restoreBackground() clears the corresponding region so the
  glyphs do not linger after the background is repainted.

The font is opt-in through the korean_ttf_font option; without it, or if
the file cannot be loaded, rendering falls back to the previous bitmap
path. Verified with the Korean fan translation of Monkey Island 1 at
both 2x and 3x, plus the bitmap fallback.
